# A poor man's version of the ContinuumSubtraction.py
# script that is an optional install for siril 1.4.
#
# Continuum Subtraction is a technique to workaround the
# fact that a narrowband filter not only lets through
# quantum emissions, but also continuous blackbody
# radiation. By using a broadband filter that contains
# the narrowband, we can subtract the background leaving
# an approximation of just the emissions.
#
# This script requires files {rgb,ha,oiii}.fit to exist
# in the working directory, i.e. for use on RGB + Ha/Oiii
# dual narrowband.
#
# Magic numbers are used to define the scaling factors
# between the narrow and broadbands.
#
# The following files are produced: delete what is not
# needed:
#
# - hgb (Ha, G, B)
# - rob (R, Oiii, B)
# - hob (Ha, Oiii, B)
# - rgb_with_ha (R + HaCS, G, B + HaCS)
# - rgb_with_oiii (R, G + OiiiCS, B + OiiiCS)
# - rgb_with_ha_and_oiii (R + HaCS, G + OiiiCS, B + HaCS + OiiiCS)
#
#
# A review of the python script reveals that it is calculating
# the median of the narrowband image (allowing user
# selection) then calculating
#
# result = narrowband - (broadband - median) * scale_factor
#
# The scale_factor is a magic number that should be manually
# (or automatically) discovered for every invocation, but it
# might be stable for a given camera/scope/gain/filter/exposure
# combination.
#
# NOTE: I think there's a bug in the python script, I think
#       it should be using the median of the continuum.
#
# and returning the result that has the scale_factor which
# minimises the Average Absolute Deviation,
# mean(abs(data - mean(data)).
#
# The following video gives a walkthrough of how to use the
# python script, and some of its other features,
# https://youtu.be/6QQlwel2dAU?si=BRD9UZ6eNTN2DLGa
#
# It is common to blend the emission lines into the RGB
# image with a specific colour, e.g. HaCS as purple, OiiiCS
# as cyan.
#
# R: R+Q*(HaCS-med(HaCS))
# G: G
# B: B+Q*(HaCS-med(HaCS))*.2
#
# G: G + Q*(OIII - med(OIII))
# B: B + Q*(OIII - med(OIII)) * 0.2
#
# Parameter: Q=0.5
#
# My general thoughts are that continuum subtraction tends to
# leave a lot of artifacts. In particular, it would be good to be
# able to remove the stars from the subtracted image because the
# pixel math doesn't do a good job of that and the composed final
# image gives the impression of chromatic abberation in the stars.

requires 1.2.0

#setcompress 1 -type=gzip1 0

load rgb
split process/r process/g process/b

load ha
save process/ha

load oiii
save process/oiii

cd process
convert channels
register channels

# siril will use alphabetical ordering, so Ha is
load r_channels_00003
save reg_ha
rgbcomp reg_ha g b -out=../hgb

#stat
#
# and this is Oiii
load r_channels_00004
save reg_oiii
rgbcomp r reg_oiii b -out=../rob

rgbcomp reg_ha reg_oiii b -out=../hob

# so that we don't have to do it again later
pm "$reg_ha$ - ($r$ - med($r$)) * 0.98"
save ha_cs

pm "$reg_oiii$ - ($g$ - med($g$)) * 0.85"
save oiii_cs

# remove the median so we don't have to repeat it out
# every time. Note that we remove the original narrowband
# median, not the median of the continuum reduced one
# like most people do.
pm "$ha_cs$ - med($reg_ha$)"
save ha_cs_m
pm "$oiii_cs$ - med($reg_oiii$)"
save oiii_cs_m

# now for the Ha
pm "$r$ + 0.5 * $ha_cs_m$"
save r_with_ha

pm "$b$ + 0.5 * 0.2 * $ha_cs_m$"
save b_with_ha

rgbcomp r_with_ha g b_with_ha -out=../rgb_with_ha

# then the Oiii
pm "$g$ + 0.5 * $oiii_cs_m$"
save g_with_oiii

pm "$b$ + 0.5 * 0.2 * $oiii_cs_m$"
save b_with_oiii

rgbcomp r g_with_oiii b_with_oiii -out=../rgb_with_oiii

# all together now
pm "$b$ + 0.5 * 0.2 * $oiii_cs_m$ + 0.5 * 0.2 * $ha_cs_m$"
save b_with_ha_and_oiii

rgbcomp r_with_ha g_with_oiii b_with_ha_and_oiii -out=../rgb_with_ha_and_oiii

cd ..
load rgb_with_ha_and_oiii

#setcompress 0